      "dataset_type": "Publication",
      "description": "Mapping the human body at single cell resolution in three dimensions (3D) is an important step toward a \u201cdigital twin\u201d model that digitizes organ structure and the dynamics of cell-cell interactions. Current approaches for 3D reconstruction of multiplexed serial sections are relatively cumbersome and unautomated. We present an integrated workflow for two-dimensional (2D) cell segmentation and 3D reconstruction for multiplexed sequential tissue sections: MATRICS-A (Multiplexed Image Three-D Reconstruction and Integrated Cell Spatial - Analysis) applied to skin from 12 donors aged 32-72 years. Using MATRICS-A, we show significantly shorter average distance between immune cells and endothelial cells 3D vs 2D (56 \u00b5m vs 108 \u00b5m) and 10-70% more T cells (total) within 30 \u00b5m of a T helper cell in 3D. The average distance of cells positive for DNA damage (p53), repair (DDB2) and proliferation (Ki67) to the skin surface was consistent with age and sun exposure, and largely localized to the lower epidermis. We present new 3D data visualization for cell distance maps and immune cell cluster density differences across samples. MATRICS-A provides a new powerful integrated open access approach to quantify 3D spatial cell relationships in healthy and aging organs and could be further extended to diseased organs.",
